{
  "result": "There is a drawer in almost everyone's house that has no real purpose. You know the one. It holds dead batteries, a takeout menu from a restaurant that closed in 2019, three Allen keys, a birthday candle, some twist ties, and a single, inexplicable key that doesn't open anything you currently own. It's called a junk drawer, but I think it's actually an archaeological record.\n\nArchaeologists in the future will not learn much about us from our carefully curated bookshelves. Those are performances. They will learn everything from the junk drawer. It shows what we actually feared losing. We kept the extra screw that came with the IKEA dresser just in case. We kept the fortune from a cookie that said 'A thrilling adventure awaits you soon' because on that day we needed to believe it.\n\nI cleaned mine out last Sunday, which is probably a sign of some deeper restlessness. I found a movie ticket stub from 2017. I don't remember the movie, but I remember who I went with, and how cold it was after, and how we walked home anyway because we didn't want the night to end.\n\nI almost threw it away. I put it back. Some things deserve to stay useless a little longer.\n\nThat is the whole secret, I think. Not everything needs to be useful or optimized or minimal. Some things just need to be kept, quietly, in the dark, until you need to remember how you felt at 4:30pm in October.",
